# Addon Data Lifecycle

- Keep schema revisions under the owning addon's `api/migrations/versions/`; never place addon changes in the shared runtime migration location.
- When tables were first created on another addon's branch (for example `system`), leave that historical revision untouched and open an owning-addon bridge revision (`down_revision=None`, `branch_labels`, `depends_on` the create revision or last mutation of those tables, no-op upgrade) for future schema work.
- Use migrations for schema changes and one-time historical data transformations. Use `BaseSeed` components for desired reference or default state that must be reconciled at startup.
- Generate revisions with the addon-aware `addon-db revision <addon>` command after ensuring all affected models are loaded through the addon import chain.
- Treat autogeneration as a draft. Review table ownership, operations, stable constraint/index names, `revision`, `down_revision`, branch metadata, data safety, and downgrade behavior.
- Keep revision IDs unique and never rewrite an applied migration. Add a new corrective revision instead.
- Do not introduce desired-state records in new migrations merely because historical migrations did so. One-time backfills belong in migrations; ongoing defaults belong in seeds.
- Make every seed safe to run repeatedly. Query by stable natural keys, create missing records, and update only fields whose desired value is owned by the addon.
- Never assume an empty database, blindly insert duplicates, or overwrite user-owned configuration from a seed.
- Keep seed transaction behavior explicit and roll back on failure. Do not emit external effects before persistence succeeds.
- Respect startup order: models must be imported before migrations are generated or run, and migrations complete before registration-time seeds.
- Test migrations against realistic prior schema state and run seeds more than once to verify idempotency.
- **Addon rename cutover (required):** renaming `addons/<old>/` → `addons/<new>/`
  (or changing stable model/settings/job identity tokens) is not done when the
  package and constants move. Seeds will insert the *new* rights/settings/jobs
  while old DB rows (model keys, categories, binding keys, paths), report
  targets, and object-store key prefixes keep the old name. Deslop and
  isolation only scan git trees — they will not catch this. After every
  rename, run
  `python3 scripts/addon/check_addon_rename_cutover.py --from <old> --to <new>`
  (add `--apply` only after reviewing the inventory). Exit 0 is the done gate.
  Policy examples use invented names only (`alpha` → `beta`).
